## Singleton Pattern

## Intent

Guarantee a class has exactly one instance and expose it via a single access point.

## Classic Implementation

```typescript
class Logger {
  private static instance: Logger;
  private logs: string[] = [];

  private constructor() {} // prevent external instantiation

  static getInstance(): Logger {
    if (!Logger.instance) {
      Logger.instance = new Logger();
    }
    return Logger.instance;
  }

  log(message: string) {
    this.logs.push(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${message}`);
    console.log(message);
  }
}

// Usage
const loggerA = Logger.getInstance();
const loggerB = Logger.getInstance();
console.log(loggerA === loggerB); // true — same instance
```

## JavaScript's Natural Singleton: Module Exports

```typescript
// supabase.ts — imported once, shared everywhere
import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js';

export const supabase = createClient(
  process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
  process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY!
);
// Every import of this module receives the same client instance
```

## Singleton in React

```typescript
// Redux store — one store per app
const store = configureStore({ reducer: rootReducer });
export default store; // module-level singleton

// Zustand store — also a singleton
export const useAuthStore = create<AuthState>((set) => ({
  user: null,
  setUser: (user) => set({ user }),
}));
```

## Problems with Singleton

```
1. Hidden global state — hard to reason about
2. Tight coupling — consumers depend on the concrete class
3. Testing — you can't swap in a mock without patching the module
4. Concurrency — (less relevant in JS, but matters in multi-threaded envs)
```

## Better Alternative: Dependency Injection

```typescript
// ❌ Ambient singleton (hard to test)
function getUser(id: string) {
  return supabase.from('users').select().eq('id', id);
}

// ✅ Injected (testable)
function getUser(id: string, client = supabase) {
  return client.from('users').select().eq('id', id);
}
// In tests: pass a mock client
```

